// ★★ THE COMPUTED DESK — filter over values the MACHINE computed, not values you typed.
//
// `demos/flt-lambda-lab/04-desk.rho` filters three Church numerals into @"numerals" and then
// pattern-matches them. Every one of those numerals is a CONSTANT someone transcribed: the
// program proves that the FLT pattern matcher works, and proves nothing at all about the
// machine's ability to compute the thing being matched.
//
// This program removes the transcription. The `[*]` suffix on a send says:
//
//     x!(P)[*]   ≡   explore EVERY execution path of P, and deliver each path's terminal
//                    term to the channel x
//
// so the payloads that arrive on @"results" are normal forms the f1r3node machine β-reduced,
// one enumerated communication at a time, inside a speculative tuplespace. Nothing below
// spells out an answer.
//
//   plus ≡ λm.λn.λf.λx. m f (n f x)     two ≡ λf.λx. f (f x)     three ≡ λf.λx. f (f (f x))
//   mult ≡ λm.λn.λf. m (n f)
//
//   plus 2 3  ⟶β  λf.λx. f (f (f (f (f x))))          the Church numeral 5
//   mult 2 3  ⟶β  λf.λx. f (f (f (f (f (f x)))))      the Church numeral 6
//
// HOW TO RUN
// ──────────
//   target/debug/rholang demos/flt-lookahead/01-computed-desk.rho
//
// WHAT HAPPENS, IN ONE ROUND
// ──────────────────────────
//   1. each `[*]` lowers to a speculation REQUEST — `@"^spec-all"!(⟦P⟧, @"results")` — and
//      NOT to a send of P on @"results". The subject is never deposited; only computed
//      terminal terms ever rest there;
//   2. an installed system process on `^spec-all` consumes the request, funds a fresh
//      speculative sandbox from this deploy's phlogiston, injects the λ guest's own installed
//      Rho-net program alongside a seed over ⟦P⟧, and enumerates every enabled rendezvous
//      breadth-first until the branch reaches quiescence;
//   3. each branch's terminal term is produced back onto @"results" — as a BARE term, so the
//      ordinary Foreign Language Term receive pattern below matches it verbatim;
//   4. the `for` matches in the SAME reduction round. There is no second pass.
//
// The two `[*]` sends are independent, so both are served; the single `for` consumes one of
// the two results and the other is left resting, exactly as it would be for two ordinary
// sends of two ordinary values.
//
// EXPECT: @"OUT" carries the BODY of one computed numeral — five or six nested applications —
// and the run reports `lookahead: 2 request(s) served`. If the engine were ever missing, the
// request would REST on ^spec-all and the interpreter would say so; it can never quietly
// degrade to publishing nothing, and it can never publish the subject back as an "answer".

@"results"!(lambda`((lam m. lam n. lam f. lam x. ((m, f), ((n, f), x)), lam f. lam x. (f, (f, x))), lam f. lam x. (f, (f, (f, x))))`)[*] |
@"results"!(lambda`((lam m. lam n. lam f. (m, (n, f)), lam f. lam x. (f, (f, x))), lam f. lam x. (f, (f, (f, x))))`)[*] |

for(@lambda`lam f. lam x. ${body}` <- @"results") {
  @"OUT"!(lambda`${body}`)
}
